<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bechuanaland Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1885 CE – 1966 CE · capital Mafeking</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate over the Tswana kingdoms, ruled from outside its own borders, which became independent Botswana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molopo-river">Molopo River</a> in 1885, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-expedition">Warren Expedition</a>, to block German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> expansion and keep open the road to the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana</a> rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bangwato-kingdom">Bangwato Kingdom</a> welcomed protection against the Boers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Administration was minimal and ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafeking</a>, outside the territory. In 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebele-i">Sebele</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathoen-i">Bathoen</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and persuaded the government not to hand them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes's</a> company. Chiefs kept wide powers, and many men worked in South African mines. The marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a> to a white Englishwoman in 1948 led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to exile him under South African pressure.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a> returned to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana-democratic-party">Bechuanaland Democratic Party</a>, which won the 1965 election. The territory became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> on 30 September 1966, with a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaborone">Gaborone</a> and Seretse Khama as president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bechuanaland-protectorate.jpg" alt="Bechuanaland Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1885 CE – 1966 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mafeking</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khama III, Tshekedi Khama, Seretse Khama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Tswana traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bechuanaland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=bechuanaland-protectorate" aria-label="Bechuanaland Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5368 3085 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bechuanaland Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5688 3341L4997 651M5688 3341L5708 3283M5688 3341L5678 3332M5688 3341L5672 3353M5688 3341L5618 3188"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5708 3283h0M5678 3332h0M5672 3353h0M5618 3188h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5688 3341h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=bechuanaland-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

